The provided output appears to be a list of buttons on an Android screen. The `resource_id` field is empty for most buttons, indicating that they do not have any associated resources.

To extract the text from these buttons, we can iterate through the list and access the `text` property of each button:

```java
List<Button> buttons = ...; // assuming this is a list of Button objects

for (Button button : buttons) {
    if (!button.getText().toString().isEmpty()) {
        System.out.println(button.getText());
    }
}
```

This code will print out the text of each non-empty button.

Alternatively, we can use Java 8's `Stream` API to achieve the same result in a more concise way:

```java
buttons.stream()
       .filter(button -> !button.getText().toString().isEmpty())
       .map(Button::getText)
       .forEach(System.out::println);
```

This code uses the `stream()` method to create an iterable stream from the list of buttons, then filters out any buttons with empty text using the `filter()` method. It then maps each button's text to a string using the `map()` method, and finally prints out the resulting strings using the `forEach()` method.
